## Decisions

1. Added `LocalArtifactRepositoryV5.authenticate_exact` and `load_evaluation_panel_spec_exact`. Both retain canonical-path confinement and digest authentication, while missing references fail at their exact paths. Existing JSON `authenticate` and legacy panel callers retain their relocation diagnostics. Raw graph edges already use the exact stream authenticator and remain unchanged.
2. Switched every baseline JSON graph/root/profile caller, both baseline panel-loading paths, and the pending authority wrapper to exact authentication. Domain parsing still follows the complete graph pass; only structural reference decoding selects the closed graph beforehand. No downstream manifest or baseline-parent contract/serialization changes were made.
3. Each transport invocation now creates an owned `policy` child next to its owned input/output/config directories. It writes the authenticated `inputs.source` UTF-8 LF bytes with create-only primitives, verifies bytes and digest after opening, and holds the existing deny-write/delete file pins until container cleanup. All four candidate mounts refer to those flat overlay files and are read-only. The actual policy checkout is never written.
4. Revalidate overlay pins before container creation and after receiving output. Keep network disabled, root filesystem read-only, image pull disabled, bounded resources, fresh invocation names, and existing source guards. Release pins before exact owned-tree removal. Cleanup attempts continue after a failed resource close, and container/removal/close failures cannot report clean completion.
5. Direct Windows verification exposed a consequential metadata issue in the reused readiness pin: Python 3.13 `lstat` and `fstat` can return different `ctime` values on the same newly written file. The narrowly scoped support repair retains full separate path and descriptor metadata snapshots, explicitly requires matching `(st_dev, st_ino)`, and rechecks each view before and after hashing. It does not omit timestamps or relax inode identity. The existing production opener binds before/opened/after file identity and holds Windows `FILE_SHARE_READ` pins that deny writes and deletes.

